{"items":[{"path":"tools.python","description":"Execute Python in a persistent per-session kernel. Variables, imports, functions, and loaded data survive across calls AND context compaction — the kernel process keeps running independently of the conversation. Use for multi-step, bulk, or programmatic work of any kind: file finding (pathlib/glob), parsing (ast/json/re/csv/tomllib/sqlite3), bulk multi-file transforms, running checks/tests/git via subprocess, and data/log analysis. The namespace starts empty — write your own reusable helper functions and call them again in later calls. Keep bulk data in variables and print only slices/summaries (output is truncated). Full stdlib is available, plus any third-party packages installed in the configured interpreter. Prefer native read/grep/edit for quick one-off lookups and surgical single-file edits: they keep OpenCode's diff display and per-path edit permissions, which kernel-side writes bypass. The trailing expression's value is returned as '=> <repr>'; use print() for output. Pass reset:true to discard all state; timeout is in seconds.","signature":"tools.python({
  /** Python code to execute in the persistent session kernel. */
  code: string,
  /** Optional execution timeout in seconds (default 120, capped at 600). */
  timeout?: number,
  /** Restart the kernel before executing, discarding all state. */
  reset?: boolean,
}): Promise<string | null>"}],"remaining":0,"next":null}

